data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 11 (Cosmos provider), the **sync-over-async opt-in is removed entirely**. All synchronous LINQ operators — `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, `SaveChanges()`, etc. — now throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime. There is no configuration to re-enable them.

### The fix: convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

### Key points

- **`Add()` is fine** — it's an in-memory operation, no sync-over-async involved.
- **All query materializers** (`ToList`, `First`, `Single`, `Count`, `Any`, etc.) must use their `*Async` counterparts.
- **`SaveChanges()`** → `SaveChangesAsync()`.
- Callers must propagate `async/await` up the call stack (controllers, services, etc.).

This change only affects the **Cosmos DB provider**. The relational providers (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, etc.) still support synchronous APIs.
